+# PocketRisu immutable execution context C4 validation
+
+Date: 2026-09-14 KST
+
+Status: **historical C4 capture checkpoint; the later H2 transport/consumer
+slice is recorded in `docs/pocketrisu-host-context-h2-validation.md`**
+
+The statements below describe implementation commit `694c6e8` at its original
+checkpoint. They are retained as the evidence for that slice, not as the
+current implementation boundary. H2 subsequently added authenticated
+transport, durable prepare binding/recovery, captured-only production
+consumers, startup reconciliation, and explicit context release while keeping
+product activation disabled.
+
+## Scope and authority
+
+Implementation commit `694c6e8` adds the first C4 execution-context owner to
+the Archive Center Go service. It adds:
+
+- an explicit PocketRisu device allowlist matching the settings currently sent
+  by the JavaScript `/prepare-turn` and `/config/update` builders;
+- one owner identity bound to host, character, chat, binding epoch, operation,
+  and claim epoch;
+- a process-memory registry that captures the device snapshot, backend runtime
+  configuration, and `memory-preprocessing.json` together;
+- exact replay/conflict and owner-bound load semantics;
+- a secret-independent public settings digest and a separate internal full-
+  material fingerprint;
+- per-context, entry-count, and aggregate memory ceilings; and
+- focused concurrency, mutation, secret, validation, capacity, and restart
+  tests.
+
+This is not a mounted C4 API and not a provider context yet. No HTTP route,
+capability, JavaScript caller, HostPrepare caller, LLM/embedding consumer,
+database migration, startup reconciler, live binary replacement, or paid call
+was added. The existing isolated 4.3.1 runtime was read only and remained
+ready with store, vector, and reference-vector checks true and degraded false.
+
+## Source-to-context allowlist
+
+The new device snapshot does not accept the complete plugin settings object.
+It freezes only fields already consumed by the two current builders:
+
+| Group | Captured values | Secret treatment |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| prepare narrative | guide mode/strength, apply mode, fixed-off takeover, episode interval, supervisor enabled | non-secret typed values |
+| prepare memory lifecycle | memory transport, finalization mode, budget mode, exact seven class budgets, recent reference count, objective item cap | non-secret typed values |
+| prepare injection/reference | injection toggle/cap, original-work cap/limit/toggle, lorebook mode/cap, Canon Base cap, input-context cap | non-secret typed values |
+| prepare presentation/hierarchy | narrative support cap, publisher format, chapter/arc/saga intervals | non-secret typed values |
+| main and critic | provider, endpoint, model, timeout, temperature, token/reasoning controls, request overrides | API key, endpoint, and extra JSON remain private material |
+| supervisor | current JavaScript derives it from main settings | no second device authority is invented |
+| embedding | provider, endpoint, model, timeout | API key and endpoint remain private material |
+| source search | provider, endpoint, model, timeout, temperature, token/reasoning controls | API key and endpoint remain private material |
+| backend common | full `RuntimeConfig` plus `memory-preprocessing.json` | credentials, endpoints, override JSON, shared/role prompts remain private material |
+
+The public digest retains non-secret values and only configured/not-configured
+bits for private provider material. It does not hash API-key bytes, endpoint
+values, extra request JSON, shared prompts, or role prompts. The internal
+fingerprint covers the full material but never leaves the registry result.
+
+## Data and control flow
+
+```text
+future authenticated PocketRisu host request
+  → exact host/character/chat/binding/operation/claim identity
+  → typed device snapshot validation
+    → exact prepare field set and seven memory-budget keys
+    → enum, numeric, text, JSON-object, and size bounds
+  → Server holds RuntimeConfigMu read lock
+    → copy RuntimeConfig including pointer values
+    → read memory-preprocessing.json under the same lock
+  → encode full device + backend material in memory
+    → reject above 1 MiB per context
+    → compute internal full-material fingerprint
+    → compute public digest after private-value redaction
+  → registry mutex
+    → exact identity + exact fingerprint: return same context ID
+    → exact identity + changed material: conflict
+    → capacity: reject without eviction
+    → otherwise create random 192-bit context ID and retain one byte copy
+  → internal load requires exact context ID and complete owner identity
+  → a new process has an empty registry and returns unavailable
+```
+
+The final API/provider path is intentionally absent. A later caller must use
+the stored material rather than calling `runtimeConfigSnapshot()`,
+`loadMultiAgentSettings()`, or `completeTurnExtractionConfig()` again.
+
+## Runtime audit v2
+
+### Phase 1 — flat discovery
+
+- every `NewServer` allocates an empty execution-context registry;
+- the registry has no route or provider caller;
+- a future capture can contain API keys, custom endpoints, extra headers/body,
+  shared prompts, and role prompts;
+- the device allowlist contains 21 prepare settings plus four presentation/
+  hierarchy settings and four provider groups;
+- supervisor device settings are derived from the main group rather than
+  duplicated;
+- full material is JSON encoded before registry insertion;
+- identity and full material produce an internal fingerprint;
+- public digest material is separately redacted;
+- configured-presence booleans remain while private values are removed;
+- same identity can be captured concurrently;
+- exact replay and changed-material replay have different results;
+- load accepts a context ID plus a complete owner identity;
+- caller mutation after capture can change nested maps and pointer-backed
+  runtime values unless the stored representation is independent;
+- runtime config and preprocessing are written through the same mutex but
+  originate from memory and a file respectively;
+- the configuration file is read while the mutex is held;
+- one context has a byte cap and the registry has count/aggregate caps;
+- capture performs normalization and encoding before the registry capacity
+  lock, so concurrent rejected attempts can allocate temporary buffers;
+- there is no release, terminal tombstone, TTL, or compaction method;
+- process restart discards all context bytes and identities;
+- Go strings/JSON buffers cannot promise immediate secret-memory zeroing after
+  garbage collection;
+- no database, socket, goroutine, timer, file write, or external provider is
+  created by the registry;
+- `HostPrepareRegistry` already accepts opaque context ID/settings digest but
+  is not connected to this owner;
+- current provider and complete paths still reread global runtime settings;
+- the internal structs are not yet an externally decoded unknown-field-
+  rejecting DTO;
+- server construction and linked binary reachability change even with an empty
+  registry.
+
+### Phase 2 — external anchors
+
+| Leaf | Break attempted | External anchor / observed result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Concurrent capture creates several context IDs | Launch sixteen captures for the same identity and material | `pocketrisu_execution_context.go:214-259` serializes lookup/insertion; the race-enabled fixture observed one non-reused owner, fifteen exact reuses, one stored context, and one context ID. |
+| A retry silently adopts changed settings | Reuse one identity with a different device secret | lines 216-225 compare the internal full-material fingerprint and returned `execution_context_fingerprint_conflict`; the first bytes and ID remained unchanged. |
+| Caller mutation changes captured settings | Mutate the input budget map, device key, backend key, and preprocessing role after capture | lines 198-208 encode material before insertion, line 252 stores an independent byte copy, and lines 296-302 decode a fresh value; the fixture recovered only the original fields. |
+| Another operation borrows the context | Load a real context ID with a different chat identity | lines 272-295 normalize and compare the complete owner identity; the fixture returned `execution_context_owner_conflict` and no material. |
+| A restart uses current settings as fallback | Create a fresh registry and load the old ID | the registry constructor at lines 159-166 creates empty maps; the fixture returned `execution_context_unavailable` with no material. |
+| Private values affect the public digest | Change every synthetic device/backend secret while keeping public values and configured-state equal | lines 544-670 blank device/runtime keys, endpoint values, extra JSON, and preprocessing prompt/endpoint/key values before hashing. Two independent fixtures produced the same public digest; changing the public main model changed it. |
+| Secret bytes are lost from the actual context | Load after capture and compare provider/runtime/preprocessing markers | full material is encoded before public redaction at lines 198-209. The internal load returned exact synthetic keys, endpoints, extra JSON, and private prompts. |
+| Unknown memory classes enter the snapshot | Add or replace a budget key | lines 403-414 require the exact seven-key allowlist; missing and extra fixtures failed before insertion. |
+| Malformed or overlarge input is retained | Try zero claim, wrong version/mode, 30,001-char reference cap, non-object provider JSON, control characters, 65,537-byte key, and over-1-MiB context | identity, enum, budget, JSON, text, and encoded-record guards rejected all nine focused cases. |
+| Global config changes after capture | Capture, then replace runtime key/model and preprocessing file prompt | `server.go:128-155` snapshots both sources under `RuntimeConfigMu`; the loaded material retained the original runtime and file values. |
+| Entry or byte capacity silently evicts a live context | Set count cap to one and aggregate cap to one stored record, then capture another identity | lines 227-231 returned capacity in both fixtures; the first context remained owner-loadable. No eviction path exists. |
+| Production can invoke the unfinished context | Search route registration, provider calls, and linked ARM64 strings | source callers are limited to the constructor, internal capture method, and tests. No execution-context route was mounted, and the linker retained no execution-context contract/route string. |
+| Existing AC runtime was changed | Read the isolated runtime after source validation | `/version` remained 4.3.1 and `/ready` remained fully ready/non-degraded. No source candidate was deployed. |
+
+### Collaborator verification processing
+
+One read-only source/wire mapping returned fifteen strict groups. The primary
+turn checked the current JavaScript builders and matching Go fields; all groups
+were usable: nine non-secret device groups, five secret-bearing device groups,
+one backend-common group, and no missing current field. It also confirmed that
+current supervisor values are derived from the main settings rather than an
+independent device source.
+
+A separate read-only ten-claim audit returned ten schema-valid YES lines. The
+primary turn checked every claim against source and tests, then added explicit
+sixteen-way concurrency, aggregate-byte capacity, and oversized-material
+fixtures before the final race run. Both collaborators made no file changes.
+
+### Phase 3 — triage
+
+- **Q1 fixed:** one context owner under concurrent capture, exact replay versus
+  changed-material conflict, complete owner-bound load, independent stored
+  bytes, and process-restart unavailable behavior.
+- **Q2/Q3 fixed:** exact device field/budget allowlist, enum/numeric/text/JSON
+  bounds, 1 MiB per context, 64 entries, 16 MiB aggregate, atomic backend
+  snapshot lock, and public private-value redaction. The audit also removed
+  preprocessing endpoint values from the public digest before commit.
+- **Q4 prepared surface:** capture normalizes/encodes before acquiring the
+  capacity mutex, so many future authenticated callers could temporarily
+  allocate up to the input ceiling. No route exists now; transport admission
+  must bound body size and concurrent capture before exposure.
+- **Product blockers:** there is no authenticated DTO/route, HostPrepare
+  binding, effective precedence resolver, provider/complete consumer, startup
+  scan, terminal release/retention, or PocketRisu host adapter.
+
+## Prepared surfaces
+
+| Item / claim | Resolved through | Exact blocked link | Why it remains open | Required next observation |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Authenticated transport | typed internal structs and result states exist | strict HTTP decode/auth/version fence → capture | route and external DTO are absent | missing/bad auth, unknown fields, legacy version, body cap, duplicate response, wrong binding/claim |
+| Durable prepare binding | result supplies context ID, digest, backend instance | capture → HostPrepare registration → start/ready/status | no caller joins the two owners | response loss/restart, changed fingerprint, ready/skip race, exact context unavailable |
+| Effective final settings | exact device and backend snapshots are frozen | existing precedence → one resolved provider/preprocessing context | both snapshots are stored but no resolver/consumer reads them | change `/config/update` and preprocessing during prepare/complete; all stages retain the first resolved values |
+| Context release | count/byte caps fail closed without eviction | every terminal/skip/fence/reference → exact release/tombstone | C6 lifetime is undefined | completion, previous-turn wait, cancel, failure, route remap, late status, compaction and ID replay |
+| Restart behavior | old context ID returns unavailable | durable registry/credential reference if unattended recovery is claimed | full secret material is intentionally process-only | restart at captured/registered/running/ready/complete boundaries with no global fallback or paid replay |
+| Public digest semantics | raw private values do not influence it | consumer must pair digest with context ID and owner identity | secret/endpoint changes with equal configured-state intentionally share a digest | changed secret/endpoint across exact and different operations; internal conflict wins, public log stays content-free |
+| Memory/latency | structural 1 MiB/64/16 MiB caps and stats exist | representative capture/load RSS, lock delay, rejected-concurrency cost | no production distribution or caller exists | synthetic size/concurrency ladder before route admission; reduce cap or preflight if needed |
+| Private memory lifetime | bytes never enter DB/HTTP/log in this slice | exact terminal zero/release and crash-dump policy | Go string/buffer GC is not secure erasure | lifecycle inspection plus documented process-memory threat boundary |
+
+## Cross-piece interaction check
+
+- `HostSessionExecutionStore` already owns binding and claim epochs; the new
+  identity carries the same dimensions but does not bypass or invoke it.
+- `HostPrepareRegistryStore` already fingerprints an opaque execution-context
+  ID and settings digest; the new result shape can supply those values after a
+  future authenticated join.
