+# PocketRisu host prepare registry C0 validation
+
+Date: 2026-09-13 KST
+
+Status: **durable prepare registry store primitive validated; HTTP, provider,
+and Node disposition integration remain unimplemented**
+
+## Scope and authority
+
+This is the C0-D experiment for the PocketRisu server chat-save integration
+plan. It starts from the prior C0-C head `701f1ad` and records implementation
+commits `31e3651`, `d15acbd`, and `043b587`. It adds only:
+
+- additive MariaDB migration 016 for one durable prepare record per fixed key;
+- an optional store interface for register, start, ready, known failure,
+  outcome unknown, skip, and status;
+- exact route, execution-claim, and host-change watermark fences;
+- skip-driven execution release and host-change-driven prepare fencing; and
+- source-shape, SQL-mock, and explicitly disposable MariaDB tests.
+
+No HTTP route, provider call, provider credential, host capability, Archive
+Center JavaScript change, PocketRisu change, startup reconciler, patched
+runtime installation, or paid operation is part of this slice.
+
+## Runtime audit Phase 1 — flat discovery
+
+- Migration 016 creates one `host_prepare_requests` row per fixed prepare key.
+- The key hashes host instance, binding epoch, operation, claim epoch, and main
+  request ID.
+- The request fingerprint additionally binds character/chat identity,
+  execution context, settings digest, input receipt, source revision, semantic
+  pre-injection payload hash, request type, and protocol version.
+- Retry time, HTTP trace identity, and response time are absent from the key
+  and fingerprint.
+- Registration accepts only the proposed PocketRisu prepare protocol and main
+  `model` requests; auxiliary request types are rejected.
+- SHA-256 fields are normalized to lowercase and malformed digests are
+  rejected.
+- Registration locks the prepare key, current route, and exact execution claim
+  in a serializable transaction.
+- Registration replays an exact existing row before rejecting a later route
+  change or missing binding.
+- The same key with a different semantic fingerprint returns conflict without
+  changing the stored row.
+- Registration requires the active slot's safe host-change watermark to cover
+  both its required and ingested boundaries.
+- A pending host mutation returns `host_changes_not_safe` and creates no
+  prepare row.
+- Start is permitted only from `registered_not_started` and increments a
+  durable run epoch by compare-and-set.
+- Exactly the transaction that changes the row to `running` returns
+  `start_authorized=true`; running replays never grant start authority.
+- A running replay with another external operation ID returns conflict.
+- Start rereads the current route, exact claim, and host-change safety
+  watermarks before authorization.
+- Ready completion accepts only the exact running epoch and an at-most-1-MiB
+  valid JSON result whose SHA-256 matches the compact stored bytes.
+- Ready completion rereads route, claim, and host-change safety before
+  publishing the result.
+- The exact ready result is returned after response loss and store restart
+  without granting another start.
+- A different result or run epoch under the same prepare key returns conflict.
+- Known failure stores an error code, retryable flag, and explicit
+  `started`/`not_started` external-call disposition.
+- A known failure does not automatically authorize another run.
+- A running record can be changed explicitly to `outcome_unknown`; this state
+  stores an `unknown` call disposition and never authorizes automatic replay.
+- A late ready or failure result against unknown/skipped/fenced state is not
+  stored or returned as ready.
+- Skip can terminalize registered, running, ready, known-failed,
+  outcome-unknown, or fenced-but-unreleased preparation.
+- Skip clears stored ready JSON/hash and retains the immutable prepare key,
+  request fingerprint, skip event, and reason.
+- Skip inserts an immutable `ac_skipped_prepare_unavailable` execution outcome
+  and releases only the exact operation/claim epoch in the same transaction.
+- Skip persists whether it released the claim so an ACK-loss replay returns
+  the same release disposition.
+- The same skip event and reason replay idempotently; a changed skip identity
+  returns conflict.
+- A stale skip after an already terminal execution cannot erase a retained
+  ready result.
+- A source-change fence that already released its claim remains fenced when a
+  late skip arrives; it does not create a second terminal outcome.
+- A timeout/ready race always converges to skipped after the authoritative
+  skip arrives, while Node still owns the final prepared-versus-skipped CAS
+  before model execution.
+- A route change before ready fences the prepare and discards the late result.
+- An ingested-but-unsafe host change prevents new prepare registration and
+  start/ready/failure/unknown transitions.
+- Applying a matched host source mutation fences every nonterminal or ready
+  prepare for the active operation, clears its result, records claim release,
+  and then stores the invalidated execution outcome.
+- Host-change safety and prepare lifecycle share the existing bounded MariaDB
+  1205/1213 transaction retry helper.
+- The registry state machine has no background timer, poller, goroutine,
+  provider client, or external lock service.
+- Running rows are not automatically discovered and changed to unknown at
+  process startup in this slice.
+- `failed_known` has no designated retry transition in this slice.
+- `expired_terminal`, result compaction, terminal retention, and payload
+  retention coupling are absent.
+- The stored execution-context ID and semantic payload hash are opaque; this
+  slice does not load or validate their referenced content.
+- Ready result JSON is structurally valid and hash-bound but not yet validated
+  against a typed injection-plan/source-decision schema.
+- The 1-MiB result bound is a defensive ceiling, not a measured production
+  prepare-context size.
+- The registry is session coordination metadata and is excluded from content
+  session migration.
+- Administrative reset/delete behavior for registry rows is not defined in
+  this slice.
+- No HTTP handler or production caller invokes the optional registry methods.
+- The registry can contain derived context/result content but no credential is
+  introduced or read by this store implementation.
+
+## Runtime audit Phase 2 — external anchors
+
+### Fixed key and semantic fingerprint
+
+Type: structural and measured.
+
+Breaking scenarios: a provider retry adds another injected payload and changes
+the idempotency identity, or a different input/settings snapshot reuses the
+same key. `HostPrepareKey` uses only the five stable execution identity fields.
+The separately hashed request fingerprint includes every semantic observation
+listed by the plan. The normalization test changes each semantic field and
+observes the same key with a different fingerprint; changing the main request
+changes the key. Uppercase digest input normalizes to the same fingerprint.
+
+Anchors:
+
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:161`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:173`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare_test.go:16`
+
+Failure behavior: invalid IDs, zero claim epoch, auxiliary request type, wrong
+protocol, malformed digest, or control characters fail before database I/O.
+
+### Registration and exactly-one start authorization
+
+Type: structural and measured.
+
+Breaking scenario: two workers register/start the same key concurrently and
+both interpret `running` as permission to call a paid provider. Registration
+locks the fixed key and verifies the current route and exact claim. Start uses
+a conditional update from `registered_not_started`, increments run epoch, and
+sets the ephemeral `start_authorized` result only for the successful update.
+Every replay returns the stored running state with authorization false.
+
+The real MariaDB test launched two registrations and two starts concurrently.
+Each pair converged on one durable row/run epoch, and exactly one start result
+reported both non-reused ownership and `start_authorized=true`.
+
+Anchors:
+
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:292`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:361`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:406`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare_integration_test.go:17`
+
+Failure behavior: select, insert, conditional-update, row-count, or commit
+failure returns an error and rolls back. Only MariaDB 1205/1213 retries the
+complete immutable transaction, with a fixed maximum of three attempts.
+
+### Ready result durability and response recovery
+
+Type: structural and measured.
+
+Breaking scenarios: a ready response is lost and a retry repeats paid work, or
+the same run epoch stores a different context. The result is compacted,
+bounded, hash-checked, and written only from the exact running epoch. A ready
+replay compares both JSON bytes and hash, and start never grants authority from
+ready state.
+
+The disposable test committed a synthetic ready plan, closed the store,
+reopened it, recovered the same compact JSON/hash, and replayed completion
+without another start. A changed registration fingerprint was rejected.
+
+Anchors:
+
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:273`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:430`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare_test.go:72`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare_integration_test.go:17`
+
+### Known failure and restart outcome-unknown
+
+Type: structural and measured.
+
+Breaking scenario: a process restarts with a running paid preparation and
+interprets the missing HTTP response as permission to execute again. The
+registry preserves running across store reconnect. An explicit recovery
+transition changes only that run epoch to `outcome_unknown`, records external
+call disposition `unknown`, and refuses late ready publication. Known failures
+separately retain whether the external call was known started.
+
+The real test observed running after reopen, changed it to unknown, rejected a
+late ready result, and then terminalized it through one durable skip. A second
+operation stored and recovered `failed_known`, retryable true, and
+`not_started` without granting a second run.
+
+Anchors:
+
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:494`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:560`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:704`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare_integration_test.go:17`
+
+The exact startup enumerator remains a prepared surface: this slice proves the
+durable state and transition, not automatic process-start reconciliation.
+
+### Skip fence, terminal ownership, and timeout race
+
+Type: structural and measured.
+
+Breaking scenarios: a late ready result appears after Node chose no-injection,
+an old skip releases a newer claim, or a delayed skip erases a ready record
+after the operation already settled. Skip stores its tombstone and execution
+outcome in one transaction. Slot release includes exact host, operation, and
+claim epoch. The release result is persisted. Before changing a non-skipped
+row, skip locks any existing terminal execution outcome; a terminal operation
+causes the stale skip to be ignored or the still-running prepare to be fenced.
+
+The real test recovered the same skip/release result after reconnect, observed
+a late ready remain non-ready, raced ready against timeout skip and ended in
+skipped state with no result, and proved a later skip could not erase the ready
+result of an already model-failed terminal operation.
+
+Anchors:
+
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:620`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:856`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare_integration_test.go:17`
+
+### Binding, claim, and host-change boundary
+
+Type: structural and measured.
+
+Breaking scenarios: a ready result publishes after route revision N+1, or a
+claim acquired at host sequence N starts prepare after mutation N+1 was
+ingested but before its source fence became safe. Every start/result/failure
+transition rereads the current route and exact slot. Preparation requires
+`safe >= required` and `safe >= ingested`. A matched host mutation also fences
+all prepare rows for the exact active operation before releasing the claim.
+
+The disposable tests observed registration rejected with
+`host_changes_not_safe` at `ingested=1/safe=0`, a ready prepare cleared and
+fenced by source invalidation, and a different running prepare fenced when the
+route epoch changed before ready. A subsequent skip converted that unreleased
+fence into a durable skip tombstone and released the exact old claim.
+
+Anchors:
+
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:783`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare.go:828`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_change.go:907`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_change_integration_test.go:203`
+
+### Schema, reachability, resources, and security
+
+Type: structural, measured, and surfaced.
+
+Migration 016 has a primary prepare key, a unique host/skip-event key, bounded
+status and external-call checks, and indexes for session/status and
+host/operation lookup. The production schema loader applied all sixteen files
+and 147/147 statements plus 103 compatibility statements twice. Readback
+observed 86 tables, 33 prepare columns, four indexes, all three CHECK clauses,
+and `CHECK TABLE ... OK`.
+
+The registry is an explicit session-migration metadata exclusion while the
+content manifest remains v4. Production source search found only the optional
+interface/method definitions, and the final linked backend retained zero
+method symbols. There is no reflection registry, command, or HTTP handler that
+can invoke the methods.
+
+All identifiers and digests are bounded before parameterized SQL. Result JSON
+is bounded before compaction and hashing. Transaction retry timers stop, and
+the state machine opens no socket, file, provider client, or background loop.
+The result JSON may contain derived prompt context; later transport must apply
+existing authorization and redaction policy rather than treating hash binding
+as secrecy.
+
+Anchors:
+
+- `migrations/016_host_prepare_registry.sql:8`
+- `go-service/internal/store/session_migration_manifest.go:278`
+- `go-service/internal/store/session_migration_manifest_test.go:184`
+- `go-service/internal/store/mariadb_host_prepare_schema_test.go:9`
+
+## Runtime audit Phase 3 — triage
+
+### Fixed in C0-D
+
+- Q3: a replayed `running` state could be mistaken for paid-work permission;
+  only the successful registered-to-running CAS now returns
+  `start_authorized=true`.
+- Q3: a boolean external-call field represented running as definitely not
+  started even though the call may begin after authorization; the durable
+  disposition is now `not_recorded/not_started/started/unknown`.
+- Q3: skip release was returned only in memory, so an ACK-loss replay could
+  report false; `execution_claim_released` is now durable.
+- Q3: a late skip could clear a ready result after the execution had already
+  settled; skip now locks the terminal outcome and leaves the result intact.
+- Q3: route/claim equality alone permitted prepare while a newer host event was
+  ingested but unsafe; every prepare authorization now checks both required and
+  ingested watermarks against safe.
+- Q3: source invalidation fenced the execution owner but not a ready prepare
+  result; the same transaction now clears and fences every prepare row for the
+  invalidated operation/claim.
+- Q3: a prepare fenced on binding change rejected later skip before releasing
+  its still-active old claim; fenced rows with no release receipt can now
+  transition to skipped, while already-released source fences remain unchanged.
+
+### Prepared surfaces
+
+1. **Authenticated HTTP and capability contract**
+   - Resolved: durable store states, reason codes, and exact lookup identity
+     exist; production caller count is zero.
+   - Missing link: no authenticated versioned prepare/status/skip route or
+     converted-binding capability gate exists.
+   - Limitation: PocketRisu cannot use this registry and legacy callers are not
+     yet separated.
+   - Review trigger: C4 must add typed DTOs and routes together, then prove
+     wrong host/session/claim/fingerprint and legacy bypass are rejected.
+
+2. **Actual provider owner and startup reconciliation**
+   - Resolved: only one start is authorized and running survives reconnect;
+     unknown never authorizes replay.
+   - Missing link: no provider worker calls Start before paid I/O, stores ready
+     after it, or enumerates running rows on process start.
+   - Limitation: the test simulates provider state transitions without a paid
+     or recoverable external operation.
+   - Review trigger: C4 must connect the actual prepare owner and prove process
+     stop at registered/running/ready boundaries without duplicate calls.
+
+3. **Typed execution context and result semantics**
+   - Resolved: context ID, settings/input/source/payload digests, result bytes,
+     and result hash are immutable.
+   - Missing link: referenced execution context and result JSON are not checked
+     against a typed injection-plan/current-input/source-decision schema.
+   - Limitation: hash consistency alone does not prove semantic correctness.
+   - Review trigger: C4 must persist/load the immutable context and validate
+     ready/empty/suppressed meanings before model use.
+
+4. **Known-failure retry transition**
+   - Resolved: known failure distinguishes call `started` from `not_started`.
+   - Missing link: no narrowly authorized failed-known-to-registered transition
+     exists.
+   - Limitation: even a proven pre-call failure currently requires later
+     policy/settle rather than automatic retry.
+   - Review trigger: C4 must define retryable error classes and prove only
+     `not_started` can receive another run epoch.
+
+5. **Node prepared-versus-skipped CAS**
+   - Resolved: Go skip wins final registry state and late results cannot publish.
+   - Missing link: PocketRisu does not yet persist the one-way
+     `pending→prepared|skipped` decision before model execution.
+   - Limitation: a ready response and local timeout can both be observed; Go
+     alone cannot decide whether Node already started the model.
+   - Review trigger: C1/C6 must put the disposition beside the durable command
+     and make provider send read the CAS winner.
+
+6. **Retention, terminal expiry, and administrative deletion**
+   - Resolved: full ready JSON is capped at 1 MiB and skip can clear it while
+     retaining a tombstone.
+   - Missing link: actual context size measurement, terminal replay lifetime,
+     `expired_terminal`, compaction, payload-reference coupling, and reset
+     semantics are absent.
+   - Limitation: rows and ready context grow once per preparation if a future
+     route invokes the primitive.
+   - Review trigger: C6 must tie retention to Node operation replay and prove
+     compaction cannot authorize a duplicate paid call.
+
+7. **Terminal execution plus historical ready status**
+   - Resolved: late skip cannot erase ready after terminal execution.
+   - Missing link: status does not yet return the joined execution outcome, so
+     a caller could read historical ready without seeing that the operation is
+     terminal.
+   - Limitation: the future HTTP adapter must check execution ownership before
+     applying a recovered plan.
+   - Review trigger: C4/C6 status DTO must expose or fence the joined terminal
+     disposition and test new-browser recovery.
+
+8. **Lock contention and multi-stream session policy**
+   - Resolved: observed register/start/ready-skip races converge, and 1205/1213
+     retries are bounded.
+   - Missing link: host-change source apply locks slot then prepare, while some
+     prepare transitions lock prepare then route/slot; multi-stream aggregation
+     is also absent.
+   - Limitation: unobserved high-contention frequency and cross-stream policy
+     cannot be inferred from source tests.
+   - Review trigger: C6 must run concurrent edit/ready/settle fault injection,
+     measure retries, and check all attached stream boundaries.
+
+9. **Patched process restart**
+   - Resolved: store reconnect recovers ready/running/unknown/skipped and the
+     ARM64 backend builds.
+   - Missing link: the running isolated 4.3.1 package remains unmodified and no
+     host route reaches the registry.
+   - Limitation: store reconnect is not end-to-end process recovery.
+   - Review trigger: after C4 mounts the route, repeat response loss and
+     process stop/start on the isolated stack before live integration.
+
+## Cross-piece interaction check
+
+- Migration plus store: production loader replay and real MariaDB transitions
+  use the same columns, checks, and indexes.
+- C0-B execution plus C0-D prepare: register/start bind the exact route,
+  operation, claim epoch, and source watermark; skip records a terminal outcome
+  and clears only that claim.
+- C0-C changes plus C0-D prepare: pending change blocks prepare; applied source
+  invalidation fences ready/running prepare rows before releasing execution.
+- Ready plus skip: the final registry tombstone contains no result bytes and
+  repeated skip preserves the release receipt.
+- Terminal execution plus stale skip: existing outcome wins and retained ready
+  bytes are not rewritten.
+- Restart plus unknown: persisted running state is observable, but only an
+  explicit recovery action can mark unknown; neither path grants start again.
+- Existing HTTP contracts: no prepare/complete handler or v1-v3 source
+  acceptance behavior changed, so this slice cannot be reached as product
+  support.
+- Session migration plus coordination: content manifest v4 remains unchanged
+  and registry rows are not copied as narrative content.
+
+## Verification
+
+- Thirty-five combined host-prepare/host-change/host-execution tests are
+  listed; seven are host-prepare-specific.
+- The three explicitly disposable MariaDB tests for execution, host change,
+  and prepare pass together.
+- The entire `internal/store` package passes.
+- The focused host-prepare/change/execution set passes under Go's race
+  detector.
+- The complete Go repository test suite and `go vet ./...` pass.
+- `node --check "Archive Center.js"` passes; JavaScript bytes are unchanged.
+- The final Linux arm64 build is 36,486,406 bytes with SHA-256
+  `79d03f143998bd51ecf1fb6d771e06d98a659cbd88c6325012d02e671e652e84`.
+  Go build metadata reports Go 1.26.6, `-trimpath`, `CGO_ENABLED=0`,
+  `GOOS=linux`, and `GOARCH=arm64`.
+- Implementation HEAD is
+  `043b587ef87850cc914262caa1dbc53227996c32`; its tree is
+  `9666a6c26d27808b32176ed2fdbe9a09d9e1102e`.
+- The production schema loader applied 16 migration files, 147/147 statements,
+  and 103 compatibility statements twice. Runtime readback reports 86 tables,
+  33 prepare columns, four indexes, all three CHECK clauses, and table status
+  `OK`.
+- Post-test readback reports zero prepare, stream, event, execution slot,
+  outcome, and all `c0-*` source/job/vector/chat rows. All five coordination
+  tables report `CHECK TABLE ... OK`.
+- Production source search finds no host-prepare caller, and the linked backend
+  contains zero host-prepare method symbols. This is non-reachability evidence,
+  not product support evidence.
+- The isolated runtime remains the checksum-verified unmodified 4.3.1 package;
+  it still reports backend, store, vector, and reference-vector ready with
+  `degraded=false`, and its dedicated tmux supervisor remains alive. This is
+  baseline health, not reachability of the uninstalled candidate.
+
+## C0-D verdict
+
+The MariaDB **durable prepare registry store primitive** is suitable as the
+P1-4 base for later product slices. This does not close P1-4 at product level:
+authenticated transport, actual provider ownership, startup reconciliation,
+typed context/result validation, Node disposition CAS, known-failure retry,
+retention/expiry, joined terminal status, multi-stream policy, and patched
+process restart remain explicit gates.
